1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,000
Welcome to Module Four, and to Unit Two: context is habitat.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,962
You know carrying capacity.

3
00:00:06,962 --> 00:00:10,700
Every habitat supports only so many individuals.

4
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:13,787
Below that limit, adding one more is cheap.

5
00:00:13,787 --> 00:00:16,387
Near it, crowding hurts everyone.

6
00:00:16,387 --> 00:00:21,512
Resources thin out, interference rises, and the whole population does worse.

7
00:00:21,512 --> 00:00:24,050
A language model has a habitat too.

8
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:28,912
It is called the context window, the amount of text the model can hold in view at once.

9
00:00:28,912 --> 00:00:30,937
And everything has to live there.

10
00:00:30,937 --> 00:00:32,425
Your instructions.

11
00:00:32,425 --> 00:00:34,275
The files the agent opened.

12
00:00:34,275 --> 00:00:36,850
The output of every command it ran.

13
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:38,612
Every correction you typed.

14
00:00:38,612 --> 00:00:40,637
Here is the first surprise.

15
00:00:40,637 --> 00:00:43,262
The model has no memory between turns.

16
00:00:43,262 --> 00:00:49,562
Each time you send a message, the entire conversation is sent again and read again, from the very top.

17
00:00:49,562 --> 00:00:52,025
So a long session is not just long.

18
00:00:52,025 --> 00:00:58,162
It gets more and more expensive with every turn, because everything earlier is paid for again and again.

19
00:00:58,162 --> 00:01:01,562
The second surprise is what actually fills the window.

20
00:01:01,562 --> 00:01:06,950
One of the Carnegie Mellon lecturers looked at fifteen hundred sessions of his team's coding agent.

21
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:10,025
The biggest share was not the model's own words.

22
00:01:10,025 --> 00:01:15,937
It was tool results, the contents of files and the output of commands, at well over a third.

23
00:01:15,937 --> 00:01:19,087
The model's replies to the person were about two percent.

24
00:01:19,087 --> 00:01:21,512
Providers soften the bill with a cache.

25
00:01:21,512 --> 00:01:27,425
If the beginning of the request is exactly the same as last time, that part is reused cheaply.

26
00:01:27,425 --> 00:01:29,637
But exactly means exactly.

27
00:01:29,637 --> 00:01:37,612
Put the time of day at the top of your instructions, switch models halfway through, or walk away for too long, and the cache is gone.

28
00:01:37,612 --> 00:01:40,600
And a bigger window is not a better habitat.

29
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:45,012
Models get worse at finding and using information as the window fills.

30
00:01:45,012 --> 00:01:47,175
They forget early instructions.

31
00:01:47,175 --> 00:01:53,387
One student in the lecture described telling an agent not to delete something, and it deleted it anyway.

32
00:01:53,387 --> 00:01:56,962
When the window nears its limit, the harness compacts it.

33
00:01:56,962 --> 00:02:01,250
It squeezes older history into a summary and keeps the recent part.

34
00:02:01,250 --> 00:02:04,212
That is a bit like density-dependent regulation.

35
00:02:04,212 --> 00:02:05,837
But it loses things.

36
00:02:05,837 --> 00:02:14,575
One team's compaction passed every benchmark, then in real use their agent forgot it had already opened a pull request, and opened it five times.

37
00:02:14,575 --> 00:02:17,150
So where does the metaphor fail?

38
00:02:17,150 --> 00:02:18,900
Food is eaten once.

39
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:22,250
Context is re-read on every single turn.

40
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:24,737
And crowding does not starve the model.

41
00:02:24,737 --> 00:02:25,962
It distracts it.

42
00:02:25,962 --> 00:02:28,875
In today's lab you will measure this yourself.

43
00:02:28,875 --> 00:02:36,512
You will run one deliberately crowded session, check how full the window is, compact it, and test what the agent still remembers.

44
00:02:36,512 --> 00:02:41,012
Then you will clear it and run one small, focused task, and compare.

45
00:02:41,012 --> 00:02:44,012
Open your terminal, and let's measure the habitat.

